"""The one definition of what a recurring invoice's lines are.

This exists because there were two, and they disagreed about money.

`recurring._generate_document` built the real invoice: a line per active
service at the tariff price, and -- for any service carrying a live
discount -- a second line at the negative discount amount, directly
under the plan it reduces. `preview_export._project_invoice` built the
projection finance reviews and approves: a line per active service at
the tariff price, and nothing else. It had never been taught about
discounts.

So a customer on a 100% discount was projected at full price and
invoiced at zero. The preview bundle overstated him, the grand total
overstated the run, and the approval was an approval of a number that
was never going to be billed. Worse, the approval FINGERPRINT is taken
over the projection's lines -- so a discount added, changed or removed
after approval did not change the digest, and Run's "something changed
since this was approved" guard, the whole point of the fingerprint, let
it straight through.

The fix is not to add the discount rule to the projection as well. That
is what produced two implementations in the first place, and the next
rule to arrive -- pro-rata, once-off charges, a per-service credit --
would have had to be written twice too, with the same chance of landing
in only one of them. Both callers now ask this module what the lines
are, and differ only in what they do with the answer: one saves
InvoiceItem rows, the other formats CSV cells.

Same reasoning as radiusauth.ownership and billing.overdue: when two
places have to agree about a rule, the rule gets a module.
"""
from decimal import Decimal, ROUND_HALF_UP

CENTS = Decimal("0.01")


def billable_services(customer, run_date):
    """The services a recurring invoice for `run_date` bills, AS THEY WILL
    BE ON THAT DATE (package #20; Rudolph, 2026-09-24: "System should bill
    what his service will be 1 October").

    * ACTIVE services, unless their end date (the first day WITHOUT service)
      is on or before `run_date`, or their start date is after it;
    * PENDING services booked to start themselves (activate_on) on or before
      `run_date`;
    * each at the plan it will be on: a plan change booked for on or before
      `run_date` is billed at the new plan. The service row is not changed;
      the plan to bill is carried on the in-memory object as
      `billing_tariff` and read by plan_invoice_lines below.

    On the billing date itself the morning crons have already applied all of
    these, so a normal run bills the same thing it always did. What changes
    is an early run (#19) and a preview taken before the date -- which now
    also stays valid through the morning crons instead of going stale.
    """
    from .models import Service

    services = []
    for service in customer.services.filter(
        status__in=[Service.Status.ACTIVE, Service.Status.PENDING]
    ).select_related("tariff", "pending_tariff"):
        if service.status == Service.Status.PENDING and not (
            service.activate_on and service.activate_on <= run_date
        ):
            continue
        if service.end_date and service.end_date <= run_date:
            continue
        # Not started yet (7 Oct 2026, Rudolph: a service is created but only
        # goes live two days later, and its start date is edited to match).
        # A run before the start date doesn't bill it; the part month is
        # invoiced pro rata by hand (Finance -> New invoice -> Pro rata).
        if service.start_date and service.start_date > run_date:
            continue
        if (
            service.pending_tariff_id
            and service.pending_tariff_date
            and service.pending_tariff_date <= run_date
        ):
            service.billing_tariff = service.pending_tariff
        services.append(service)
    return services


def plan_invoice_lines(services, run_date):
    """The lines a recurring invoice for `services` would carry on `run_date`.

    Returns plain dicts, ordered exactly as they must appear on the
    document: each plan immediately followed by its own discount, if one
    is live on that date.

    `run_date` is the day the invoice is raised, and it is what decides
    whether a time-limited discount applies -- these lines carry no
    period of their own to test against instead. Worth knowing when
    setting one: end a discount the day BEFORE the next billing date,
    not on it.
    """
    lines = []
    for service in services:
        # The plan in force on `run_date` -- see billable_services.
        tariff = getattr(service, "billing_tariff", None) or service.tariff
        lines.append({
            "kind": "tariff",
            "service": service,
            "tariff": tariff,
            "type": tariff.service_type,
            "description": "%s (%s)" % (tariff.name, tariff.get_billing_period_display()),
            "quantity": Decimal("1"),
            "unit_price": Decimal(tariff.price),
            "tax_rate_pct": Decimal(tariff.tax_rate_pct),
        })

        discount = service.discount_amount_for(tariff.price, run_date)
        if discount > 0:
            lines.append({
                "kind": "discount",
                "service": service,
                # No tariff on a discount line: it is not a plan being sold,
                # and hanging the tariff off it would make it count as one in
                # anything that groups by tariff.
                "tariff": None,
                "type": tariff.service_type,
                "description": service.discount_label(),
                "quantity": Decimal("1"),
                "unit_price": -Decimal(discount),
                # The SAME rate as the line it reduces, on purpose. Totals are
                # summed per line, so this contributes NEGATIVE VAT and the
                # customer pays VAT on the discounted value -- which is what
                # SARS expects, a discount reducing the taxable supply. A zero
                # rate here would charge full VAT on a reduced supply:
                # over-declaring output VAT on every discounted invoice,
                # quietly, for as long as nobody checked.
                "tax_rate_pct": Decimal(tariff.tax_rate_pct),
            })

    return lines


def billable_customer_charges(customer, run_date):
    """The customer's recurring charges (domain, email, hosting, IP rental)
    live on `run_date` -- started on or before it, not yet at their
    end_date. On the customer, not a service (Rudolph, 1 Oct), so a
    customer with one of these and no service at all is still billed.

    The catalog entry's `is_active` is NOT checked, on purpose -- same as
    Tariff.is_active: unticking it only stops new customers being given
    it. To stop billing one customer, end or delete their charge."""
    return [
        link
        for link in customer.customer_charges.select_related("charge").order_by("charge__name", "pk")
        if link.is_billable_on(run_date)
    ]


def customer_charge_lines(links):
    """One invoice line per customer recurring charge, after every service
    line, at the catalog's live price (excl. VAT) and tax rate."""
    lines = []
    for link in links:
        charge = link.charge
        description = charge.name
        if link.note.strip():
            description = f"{charge.name} — {link.note.strip()}"
        lines.append({
            "kind": "recurring_charge",
            "service": None,
            "tariff": None,
            "recurring_charge": charge,
            "type": "recurring_charge",
            "description": description,
            "quantity": Decimal(link.quantity),
            "unit_price": Decimal(charge.price),
            "tax_rate_pct": Decimal(charge.tax_rate_pct),
        })
    return lines


def customer_invoice_lines(customer, run_date):
    """Everything a recurring invoice for this customer carries on
    `run_date`: each billable service's plan (+ discount), then each live
    customer recurring charge. ONE function the engine and the preview
    projection both call, so they cannot disagree about what is billed.
    Empty means nothing to bill."""
    return (
        plan_invoice_lines(billable_services(customer, run_date), run_date)
        + customer_charge_lines(billable_customer_charges(customer, run_date))
    )


def document_totals(rows):
    """(subtotal, tax, total) for `rows` of (quantity, unit_price, tax_rate).

    Mirrors Invoice.recalc_totals EXACTLY, and has to: the projection's
    job is to predict the invoice to the cent, and the rounding ORDER is
    where that quietly stops being true.

    The invoice sums at full precision and rounds each of the two columns
    once, so the tax invoice adds up by construction. The projection used
    to round every line and add the rounded figures. On a single positive
    line those agree; introduce a negative discount line and they need
    not. A R999 plan at 15% with a 50% discount is R574.43 on the invoice
    (499.50 + 74.93) and R574.42 by per-line rounding -- one cent, on
    every such customer, between the number finance approved and the
    number billed.

    Stated once here rather than derived twice. Invoice.recalc_totals is
    still the authority -- it is what actually writes the columns -- and
    test_preview_discounts pins the two to each other so this cannot
    drift back apart unnoticed.
    """
    rows = list(rows)
    subtotal = sum((Decimal(q) * Decimal(p) for q, p, _ in rows), Decimal("0"))
    tax = sum(
        (Decimal(q) * Decimal(p) * (Decimal(r) / 100) for q, p, r in rows), Decimal("0")
    )
    # ROUND_HALF_UP, not Python's default half-even: it is what Postgres
    # does to a numeric(12,2), which is what rounds the real columns.
    subtotal = subtotal.quantize(CENTS, rounding=ROUND_HALF_UP)
    tax = tax.quantize(CENTS, rounding=ROUND_HALF_UP)
    return subtotal, tax, subtotal + tax
